> How do you convert an amount of money (say 10 reais) in PPP-equivalent terms?
**assistant**:
> To express **10 Brazilian reais (BRL)** in PPP-equivalent terms, divide it by Brazil’s PPP conversion factor for the relevant **year** and **price concept**:
> 
> PPP-adjusted international dollars=amount in BRLPPP conversion factor (BRL per international $)
> 
> For example, if the PPP conversion factor is:
> 
> 2.5 BRL per international dollar
> 
> then:
> 
> 10 BRL÷2.5=4 international dollars (PPP)
> 
> So **R10⁢ℎ⁡𝑎⁢𝑠⁢𝑡⁢ℎ⁡𝑒⁢𝑝⁢𝑢⁢𝑟⁢𝑐⁢ℎ⁡𝑎⁢𝑠⁢𝑖⁢𝑛⁢𝑔 −𝑝⁢𝑜⁢𝑤⁢𝑒⁢𝑟⁢𝑒⁢𝑞⁢𝑢⁢𝑖⁢𝑣⁢𝑎⁢𝑙⁢𝑒⁢𝑛⁢𝑡⁢𝑜⁢𝑓⁡𝐼⁢𝑛⁢𝑡4** under that PPP benchmark.
> 
> The inverse conversion is:
> 
> BRL=international dollars×PPP factor (BRL per Int$)
> 
> So Int4⁢𝑤⁢𝑜⁢𝑢⁢𝑙⁢𝑑⁢𝑐⁢𝑜⁢𝑟⁢𝑟⁢𝑒⁢𝑠⁢𝑝⁢𝑜⁢𝑛⁢𝑑⁢𝑡⁢𝑜⁢𝑅10 in the example.
> 
> A few important qualifications:
> 
> - Use the factor for the **specific year**—PPP conversion factors change over time.
> - Choose the appropriate PPP series:
>   - **GDP PPP** for comparing total economic output or GDP-valued quantities;
>   - **household consumption / individual-consumption PPP** for household purchasing power, welfare, or living standards.
> - An “international dollar” is a synthetic unit whose purchasing power is intended to match one US dollar in a reference economy/year. It is **not** the same thing as converting BRL to USD using the market exchange rate.
> 
> If instead you mean “what amount of *US dollars or another country’s currency* has the same purchasing power as R10⁢𝑖⁢𝑛⁢𝐵⁢𝑟⁢𝑎⁢𝑧⁢𝑖⁢𝑙?″,𝑡⁢ℎ⁡𝑒⁢𝑐⁢𝑙⁢𝑒⁢𝑎⁢𝑛⁢𝑒⁢𝑠⁢𝑡⁢𝑐⁢𝑜⁢𝑚⁢𝑚⁢𝑜⁢𝑛 −𝑢⁢𝑛⁢𝑖⁢𝑡⁢𝑎⁢𝑛⁢𝑠⁢𝑤⁢𝑒⁢𝑟⁢𝑖⁢𝑠⁢𝑓⁡𝑖⁢𝑟⁢𝑠⁢𝑡⁢𝑡⁢𝑜⁢𝑐⁢𝑜⁢𝑛⁢𝑣⁢𝑒⁢𝑟⁢𝑡⁢𝑅10 to **international dollars** as above. You can then convert those international dollars into a target country’s local PPP-equivalent amount using that target country’s PPP factor.
**user**:
> Okay. I've also heard about problems with first converting from reais to USD, then using the US deflator, vs. first using national deflators than then converting to USD. What are the effects of this?
**assistant**:
> The two procedures answer **different questions**, because exchange rates, Brazilian inflation, and US inflation do not generally move together.
> 
> Let:
> 
> - \(X_t\): nominal amount in reais in year \(t\)
> - \(E_t\): market exchange rate in BRL per USD in year \(t\)
> - \(P^{BR}_t\): Brazilian price index
> - \(P^{US}_t\): US price index
> - \(0\): a chosen base year
> 
> ## 1. Convert to current USD, then deflate with the US deflator
> 
> \[
> X^{USD_0}_{A}
> =
> \frac{X_t}{E_t}
> \frac{P^{US}_0}{P^{US}_t}.
> \]
> 
> This says:
> 
> > “What was this amount worth at the **market exchange rate of year \(t\)**, expressed in the purchasing power of **base-year US dollars**?”
> 
> This is usually sensible for something inherently valued in internationally traded currency or financial terms: e.g., foreign debt, export revenues, traded commodities, international investment, or a model result whose monetary numeraire is explicitly USD at market exchange rates.
> 
> But it embeds exchange-rate variation. A sharp real depreciation of the real makes the value look much smaller in USD, even if its ability to buy Brazilian goods and services was unchanged.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Deflate in reais, then convert at a base-year exchange rate
> 
> \[
> X^{USD_0}_{B}
> =
> X_t
> \frac{P^{BR}_0}{P^{BR}_t}
> \frac{1}{E_0}.
> \]
> 
> This says:
> 
> > “First, what is the amount in **constant base-year Brazilian reais**? Then what was that worth at the **base-year** BRL/USD exchange rate?”
> 
> This is more appropriate if the underlying quantity is a **domestic Brazilian real resource**—for example, local household consumption, local public expenditure, or domestic damages—where you want to remove Brazilian price inflation before expressing results in a common currency unit.
> 
> It does *not* treat later movements in the market exchange rate as changes in the real domestic quantity.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Why they differ
> 
> Their ratio is
> 
> \[
> \frac{X_A^{USD_0}}{X_B^{USD_0}}
> =
> \frac{E_0}{E_t}
> \frac{P^{BR}_t/P^{BR}_0}{P^{US}_t/P^{US}_0}.
> \]
> 
> This is essentially the change in the **real exchange rate** between Brazil and the United States.
> 
> Thus, the discrepancy reflects:
> 
> 1. **nominal depreciation/appreciation** of the real; and  
> 2. **inflation differentials** between Brazil and the US.
> 
> They would give the same answer only if exchange-rate changes exactly offset the relative inflation difference—i.e., a purchasing-power-parity-type relationship holds over the period.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Simple illustration
> 
> Suppose, from the base year to year \(t\):
> 
> - Brazilian prices doubled: \(P^{BR}_t/P^{BR}_0 = 2.0\);
> - US prices rose 20%: \(P^{US}_t/P^{US}_0 = 1.2\);
> - exchange rate moved from \(E_0=5\) BRL/USD to \(E_t=7.5\) BRL/USD;
> - the nominal amount is \(X_t=100\) BRL.
> 
> ### Convert first, then US-deflate
> 
> \[
> \frac{100}{7.5}\frac{1}{1.2}
> = 11.11 \text{ USD}_{0}.
> \]
> 
> ### Brazil-deflate first, then use the base-year exchange rate
> 
> \[
> 100 \times \frac{1}{2.0} \times \frac{1}{5}
> = 10.00 \text{ USD}_{0}.
> \]
> 
> The first approach reports about 11% more in base-year USD. That difference is not an arithmetic error: it reflects the fact that the real’s depreciation was smaller than would have been implied by the Brazil–US inflation differential.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## A common incorrect hybrid
> 
> One sometimes sees:
> 
> \[
> X_t
> \frac{P^{BR}_0}{P^{BR}_t}
> \frac{1}{E_t}.
> \]
> 
> That is, deflate to **base-year reais**, but convert them using the **current-year nominal exchange rate**. This mixes units: a constant-price real is being converted using a rate that applies to current-price reais. It can be used only if one has explicitly constructed a corresponding **real exchange rate** conversion—not merely the nominal rate \(E_t\).
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Where PPP fits
> 
> For international comparisons of **real income, consumption, welfare, or domestic production**, neither route is usually ideal. Use a PPP conversion factor:
> 
> \[
> X_t^{\mathrm{Int\$}}
> =
> \frac{X_t^{BRL}}{\mathrm{PPP}_{BR,t}},
> \]
> 
> where \(\mathrm{PPP}_{BR,t}\) is in BRL per international dollar and corresponds to the relevant aggregate (GDP, actual individual consumption, etc.).
> 
> PPP conversion is intended to compare the purchasing power of currencies over a broadly comparable basket of goods and services. Market exchange rates instead value currency in international financial markets.
> 
> So, in short:
> 
> | Objective | Usually appropriate treatment |
> |---|---|
> | Value in internationally tradable/financial USD | Convert at market exchange rate, then use a US deflator if constant USD are needed |
> | Domestic Brazilian real quantity over time | Deflate using a Brazilian price index; retain BRL or convert using a fixed base-year conversion |
> | Cross-country welfare, consumption, or real output comparison | Use the relevant PPP conversion factor |
> | Comparing model monetary outputs across time and countries | Establish the model’s numeraire first; ideally report both market-USD and PPP-based results where their interpretations matter |
> 
> The key is that “real USD” is not a neutral universal unit. It means purchasing power in the **United States**; “real BRL” means purchasing power in **Brazil**; and PPP international dollars are constructed for a third purpose: cross-country real comparisons.
